# Underscore Addressing

An underscore may follow an identifier's first character. Exact names always
win: defining 'counts_keys' makes it an ordinary variable, not an address.
The existing empty literal '_' and numeric separators such as '1_000' retain
their meanings.

For an otherwise unresolved read, 'receiver_item' can select one visible,
pure, unary source-catalog operation named 'item' whose ordinary type scheme
accepts 'receiver':

    +> map
    +> sequence
    counts := {"a": 2u64, "b": 1u64}
    keys := counts_keys
    count := keys_length

These reads lower to the same canonical operations as 'map/keys(counts)' and
'sequence/length(keys)'. There is no property runtime and no separate native
execution path. Existing result selection works, for example 'counts_keys[1u64]'.
An annotated result definition still uses ordinary conformance:

    keys<{string}> := counts_keys

Suffix matching uses the exported canonical item, not an imported alias.
For example, '+> len := sequence/length' makes 'text_length' visible and makes
'len(text)' a valid explicit call; it does not invent 'text_len'. The metadata
query reports 'length' as the item and 'len' among its visible call spellings.

The suffixes are not a fixed list. A selected catalog exporting 'table/size'
or 'matrix/length' can supply 'foo_size' or 'bar_length' through the same rule.
The current standard catalog exposes 'sequence/length'; this syntax does not
invent a missing 'matrix/length' or 'table/size' implementation.

Resolution respects imported operation visibility, concrete receiver types and
the catalog's existing kind, dimension, conversion and contract checks. Every
underscore split with a known receiver is considered. If two operations or
two splits are applicable, compilation reports their explicit qualified calls
instead of choosing a namespace or the longest receiver name.

Compiler resolution and future dot-style completion share the public
'FunctionEnvironment::receiver_members' metadata query. It reads the selected
catalog's module registrations, current import spellings and type signatures,
and returns the member name, qualified operation identity, declared signature,
structured compatibility status, and all original semantic contract variants.
The query observes new module registrations without a separate editor list.
It does not execute values or grant a runtime/native capability.

The compiler explicitly requests 'ReceiverMemberRole::UnaryPureProjection',
which remains pure, unary and single-output. Its resolved receiver conversions
and output use ordinary full-call resolution and the same selected-output-rule
consistency helper as explicit calls.

Future completion callers request 'ReceiverMemberRole::Completion'. This role
also returns visible functions requiring additional arguments, multi-output
functions and external interactions when their first source argument can be
the receiver. 'PrefixCompatible' means only that the supplied receiver prefix
passed the ordinary core constraint environment; it does NOT select a callable
overload, output schema, remaining arguments or runtime permission. Constraints
depending on unsupplied arguments are deferred, and all compatible overload
IDs remain because later arguments can change ranking. A source template
needing additional arguments reports 'ArgumentsRequired', not a promise that
the receiver is compatible. Contract metadata lets tooling describe external
interactions without pretending it knows the user's execution grants.

Either role can include hidden, unresolved and incompatible signatures for
diagnostics. Source-module roots that shadow catalog spellings are supplied
as scope metadata, as are exact callable names shadowed by document functions.
A shadowed spelling does not hide an operation with another unshadowed alias;
an exact function name is not treated as a module root. Underscore reads do
not call document functions implicitly. The editor's actual completion UI is
future work; this feature supplies its shared engine query rather than another
semantic registry.

Explicit local/state names and declared external input names take precedence.
An occurrence annotation on an unresolved complete name still declares that
exact external input; annotate a result definition to constrain an address.
Names with no recognized receiver/item pair remain ordinary identifiers.
Implicit receivers are not created, and Dynamic receivers need concrete kind
annotations.

This first form is unary read sugar only. 'receiver_item(arguments)' remains
ordinary function-call syntax: it does not insert an implicit first argument,
bind a method value, or change named-argument rules. Use an explicit call when
additional arguments are needed. Chained projections can use intermediate
bindings, as in 'keys' above.
